\section{Deciding Equality Without Evaluating Everything}

An implementation can compare weak-head forms, exposing just enough reduction to reveal a constructor, binder, or stuck expression. Matching constructors lead to recursive comparisons of corresponding arguments. To compare two function types, open their codomains with the \emph{same} fresh variable before comparing them. If two expressions are stuck on the same variable, compare their elimination spines rather than guessing what that variable will later denote. Closure environments can delay substitution, and a binder representation based on indices can make renaming invisible to the comparator. User-facing messages should nevertheless retain binder names and original type syntax.

The comparison algorithm owes two distinct facts to the calculus: each reduction it performs must preserve the judgment, and its comparison strategy must recognize every conversion it claims to decide. For a terminating, confluent fragment, comparison of normal forms up to renaming is a useful reference implementation and test oracle. A weak-head algorithm must additionally account for any admitted extensional rules; for example, function $\eta$ can make a variable and its forwarding lambda equal even though their weak-head shapes differ. Do not import such rules from an optimizer: rules valid for total pure terms need not be valid for effectful computation. Unrestricted unfolding of general recursion can also destroy termination. Real checkers make transparency and termination part of their conversion contract; if an implementation runs out of resources checking that contract, it should report that limit rather than claim to have established inequality.
