\section{Quoting Functions at a Common Fresh Argument}

In a pure, terminating simply typed core with function $\eta$,
compare terms by quoting their values back to long normal forms.
This adds something that merely reducing to constructors cannot do:
it exposes the behavior of an unknown function without assigning
it a particular input. Assume only arrows and a base type with
canonical constants, and an evaluator whose values are closures,
constants, or neutrals. A neutral is an unknown variable followed
by a list of stuck applications, each with its domain type. The
input to quotation is a typed semantic value and the current
binder depth. Its output is syntax in which a bound reference
counts intervening binders from its occurrence.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input type $T$, semantic value $v$, depth $d$
  \Output long normal form of $v$ at $T$
  \Function{Quote}{$T,v,d$}
    \If{$T=A\to B$}
      \State $x\gets\Call{Neutral}{d}$ \Comment{Fresh level}
      \State $w\gets\Call{Apply}{v,x}$
      \State \Return $\lambda.\,\Call{Quote}{B,w,d+1}$
    \ElsIf{$v$ is a constant}
      \State \Return its constant syntax
    \EndIf
    \State $(l,S)\gets\Call{HeadAndSpine}{v}$
    \State $r\gets\Call{Var}{d-1-l}$ \Comment{Level to index}
    \ForAll{$(A,a)$ in spine $S$ in application order}
      \State $r\gets r\,\Call{Quote}{A,a,d}$
    \EndFor
    \State \Return $r$
  \EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

In a context with just $f:\mathsf{Int}\to\mathsf{Int}$, reflect
$f$ as the neutral at level $0$. At depth $1$, quote both $f$
and the closure for $\lambda y.\,f\,y$. The fresh argument has
level $1$. Applying either semantic value gives head $0$ and
spine containing that argument. At depth $2$ the head quotes
to index $1$ and the argument to index $0$. Both terms thus
produce $\lambda.\,\mathsf{var}(1)\,\mathsf{var}(0)$ and compare
equal. A function returning integer $0$ instead has a constant
body and fails this equality check.

Quotation never substitutes a concrete test value for an unknown.
All levels in a quoted neutral are in scope at the supplied
depth, including those in its spine. Applying at arrow type
is sound for this example precisely because function $\eta$ is
part of the theory. Neutral arguments themselves may be functions;
printing their spines without recursively quoting those arguments
would miss conversions. Long normal forms can grow substantially,
so this simple procedure is a useful reference for smaller tests
even when a production checker compares closures incrementally.
